Coastal wetlands act as natural infrastructure, mitigating storm surge and wave energy through three primary physical mechanisms. First, vegetation density creates hydraulic drag. As storm surges and waves push through marsh grasses or mangrove roots, the friction between the water and plant stems dissipates kinetic energy, significantly reducing wave height and velocity. Second, wetlands provide vertical elevation and surface roughness. The complex root systems and dense foliage increase the surface roughness coefficient, slowing water flow and allowing sediment to settle, which further dampens energy. Third, wetlands offer storage capacity. During high tides or storm surges, wetlands act